ce here flips the opponent\'s pieces at A3 and A4 (horizontal) and B2 (vertical). Most importantly, after this move, the opponent (\'@\') will have no valid legal moves at the only remaining empty square, B3. All directions from B3 will either be blocked by my pieces without a bracketing opponent piece or immediately adjacent to an opponent\'s piece. This forces the opponent to pass.\n2.  **Move (B, 3)**: Placing a piece here would capture several pieces (flipping B2, B4, B5, A4, C4, D5). However, it leaves A2 open. The opponent would then be able to play at A2, which is a very strong move for them, flipping my piece at B2 (and potentially others in column 2) and my piece at B3 (via diagonal). Giving the opponent the last move is detrimental.\n\nReason: The optimal strategy is to play (A, 2). This move acts as a "parity" play, forcing the opponent to skip their turn because they will have no legal moves. This allows me to play at B3 on my next turn, effectively claiming both remaining spots on the board and maximizing my piece count while denying the opponent any chance to counter-attack.\n\nAnswer: (A, 2)'}
